# What never reaches the Docker daemon.
#
# Without this file the build context is everything under the repository root:
# measured here, 86 MB of node_modules, 90 MB of bin/, plus obj/ and .git, sent
# over the socket on every single build. The visible cost is the wait. The
# invisible one is worse -- the context is hashed to decide whether a COPY layer
# can be reused, so a file that changes on every local build (anything in bin/)
# invalidates layers that have nothing to do with it, and the cache never hits.
#
# A denylist, mirroring .gitignore, rather than the `*` + `!` allowlist form.
# The allowlist is stricter -- a new folder is excluded until someone says
# otherwise, where here it is included until someone remembers -- and it lost on
# readability at this size. Revisit it when the Python service adds a third
# source tree.

# --- Secrets. The build context is not the final image, but it is a layer in
# the SDK stage, and `docker build --target` can stop there ---
#
# Every one of these carries `**/`, and the reason is the difference between
# this file and .gitignore that costs a leak to learn. A .gitignore pattern with
# no slash in it matches at any depth; a .dockerignore pattern is matched against
# the whole path with Go's filepath.Match, where `*` does not cross a `/`. So
# `appsettings.Development.json` here means *the one in the repository root* and
# nothing else -- and this application's copy lives in src/LandMoney.Web/.
#
# Found by running rather than by reading: the first image built from this file
# had src/LandMoney.Web/appsettings.Development.json inside it, an untracked file
# that git has been hiding since 2026-08-05. That one turned out to hold nothing
# but log levels. The rule it broke is the one that matters, and it broke
# silently, in the direction where the file that eventually holds a connection
# string is the one nobody re-checks.
**/.env
**/.env.*
!**/.env.example
**/*.key
**/*.pfx
**/appsettings.Development.json
**/appsettings.Local.json
**/secrets/

# --- .NET build output ---
# obj/ is the one with teeth: it holds project.assets.json, written by a restore
# on *this* machine, with Windows paths and this machine's NuGet fallback
# folders baked into it. Copied into a Linux SDK stage it is not merely useless,
# it is actively wrong, and the error names a path that does not exist.
**/bin/
**/obj/
**/*.user
**/TestResults/
artifacts/

# --- Node ---
# node_modules is reinstalled inside the node stage from package-lock.json, so
# a copy of this machine's -- with win32 native bindings in it -- would be
# overwritten at best and shadow the Linux ones at worst.
**/node_modules/

# --- The client's build output, and the reason this line exists ---
# wwwroot is git-ignored, so it is invisible in a diff, and it is populated on
# this machine right now. Without this line the SDK stage starts with the last
# local `npm run build` already in place, and the node stage's COPY --from lands
# on top of it -- COPY merges directories, it does not mirror them. Old hashed
# assets are never deleted, so the image ships two bundles while index.html
# names one.
#
# The worse half is what it hides. With a stale wwwroot in the context, an image
# built with the node stage broken, skipped or reordered still serves a working
# client -- the previous one -- and nothing anywhere reports its age. That is
# exactly the failure docs/roadmap.md flags for #23, arriving through a door
# nobody was watching.
src/LandMoney.Web/wwwroot/

# --- Repository and tooling state, of no use to a build ---
.git/
.gitignore
.github/
.vs/
.vscode/
.idea/
.claude/
docs/
*.md

# --- The migration bundle -- #37 ---
#
# `efbundle` is built at the repository root, by ci.yml and by anyone following
# step 13 of docs/deploy-azure.md, and it is 128 MB of self-contained linux-x64
# runtime. It belongs to the deployment, not to the image: the image runs the
# app, and the whole reason the bundle exists is that the app does not migrate
# itself. Without this line a `docker build` on a machine that has just
# downloaded one copies it into the build context and then into the SDK stage,
# where it is invisible, weighs more than everything else in this repository put
# together, and ships.
efbundle
efbundle.exe
